{
  "id": "red-herring-proration-rounding",
  "title": "Proration regression hidden behind rate-limit, cache-timeout and ERESOLVE noise",
  "description": "PR #612 refactors src/billing/proration.ts to prorate by remainingDays() (Math.floor of the remaining time in days) instead of the exact remaining milliseconds, so a mid-day plan change loses the partial day: two unchanged tests in test/billing/proration.test.ts fail with 'expected 1700 to be 1750' (17 instead of 17.5 of 30 days). The log is full of loud, signature-matching noise in steps that SUCCEEDED: Docker Hub 'toomanyrequests' twice while pulling the postgres service image (the third pull worked and the service became healthy), a cache restore that failed with ETIMEDOUT, an npm ERESOLVE peer-dependency warning, and ETIMEDOUT/ECONNREFUSED lines printed by passing retry tests. None of it affected the result: migrations ran against the database and 33 tests passed. The signature hints all point at infrastructure or dependency, none at the real cause. Correct answer: code, flaky=false, fix the Math.floor whole-day proration in src/billing/proration.ts (docs/billing.md and the unchanged doc comment both say proration uses the exact time left).",
